limit-by-day-scichem-is-below-every-trial-arc-and-on-goldfish-the-shortfall-is-not-its-layer EXIT=0 :: Created Issue: f04a469c-ca63-478d-8ca4-feb75179bdd2
  name: limit-by-day-scichem-is-below-every-trial-arc-and-on-goldfish-the-shortfall-is-not-its-layer
  description: Known limit (HB-196 Part A item 2; HB-183, HB-185; T58 of every dense brief's TEVV page, src/hazbrief/brief/conditions.yaml): on the daytime two-phase field trials (Desert Tortoise 1 to 3, Goldfish 1 to 3; 11 arcs at 300 to 3,000 m), SCICHEM as hazbrief runs it is below every arc: MG 2.66 (95 percent 1.98 to 3.59) scored as its authors score a peak, and the mean a brief draws 4.33 (docs/evidence/hb185/statistics.tsv). On Desert Tortoise the layer the engine computes was convective where the measured one was weakly stable; given the measured Monin-Obukhov length those trials score 1.78, the stable trials' level. On Goldfish the computed layer was already stable and the shortfall (2.24) is not the layer, and it is unexplained (docs/evidence/hb185/by_material.tsv, layer.tsv). These runs were at the 250 m vertical setting; none has been repeated at the 1.25 m setting every dense brief now runs at. Whether a brief's computed layer misstates its own surface layer is not tested. A later version needs an evaluation of a brief's computed layer against observed surface-layer stability.
  type: missing_content
  quadrant: BACKLOG (importance=medium, urgency=low)
  detection: review
  target: code
